Subject: Pristine `replace` with a constant 8-bit replacement allocates the result string once at its final size

What this does
  - in `js_regexp_replace_fast`, when the replacement is a string without `$` and both the subject and the replacement are 8-bit, the result length is computed from the match vector and the string is allocated once and filled by copying the spans between matches and the replacement; an empty result is the empty string
  - functional replacements, replacements with `$`, and 16-bit subjects or replacements keep the `StringBuffer` path

Why we need it
  The `StringBuffer` grows by reallocation and is trimmed by a final realloc; `s.replace(re, '')` is 712 call sites of the regexp row. Regexp row -4.0% cycles at fixed work.

Soundness
  The same spans are copied in the same order under the same `pos >= next` rule as the buffer path, so the result bytes are identical; a result longer than `JS_STRING_LEN_MAX` throws the same RangeError the buffer would. Checked on 720 subject/pattern/replacement cases (global and not, empty matches, adjacent matches, whole-subject match, 8-bit and 16-bit subjects) against a functional replacement returning the same string: 0 differences.

